window.docContents[39].resolve({"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Using--cases--on-Expressions--Destructing-Tuples":{"contents":"The cases tactic is useful when we are dealing with values\nthat can be one of a list of things (a Bool is either a false or a true,\na Nat is either 0 or succ n, etc.). When we want more information about a\n value that is a tuple of multiple things, we instead\nwant a way to extract the pieces of that value.\n\nIf we have a value v : α × β in our context, we can\nextract the first and second components of v and give them names using this tactic:\n\nlet ⟨a, b⟩ := v\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Tactics: More Basic Tactics\u0009Using  cases  on Expressions","header":"7.2.1. Destructing Tuples","id":"/Tactics/#Logical-Foundations--Tactics___-More-Basic-Tactics--Using--cases--on-Expressions--Destructing-Tuples"}});